@meta
id: 13-07
title: The edges of the line
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-anchors
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F for PORT=, land on DB_PORT, Enter, Enter, Enter, Enter
open: .env

@brief
The API is moving to port `8080`. Change `PORT` in this env file —
just `PORT`, not the database port, the broker port, the metrics port
or the SMTP port, all of which come before it in the file and all of
which end in `PORT=`.

A search for `PORT=` lands on `DB_PORT=` and stays wrong for four more
presses of `n`. The thing that makes the right line different is not
the text, it is the position: on that line `PORT` is the first thing on
it.

Patterns can talk about position. Two characters anchor a match to the
edges of a line.

@keys
| `^` | the start of the line — matches a position, not a character |
| `$` | the end of the line |
| `/^PORT=` | `PORT=` only where it starts the line |
| `/=$` | an equals sign with nothing after it — an empty value |
| `$` then `ciw` | to the end of the line, change the word there |

@hints
Four keys end in `PORT=`. Only one *begins* with it. Say so in the
pattern.
--
`^` at the front of a pattern means "at the start of the line". It is
the same character that negated a bracket set, but outside brackets it
is an anchor.
--
`/^PORT=` `Enter` lands on the right line. `$` to the value, `ciw8080`
and `Esc` — the value is one word.

@solution
/^PORT=<Enter>  $ciw8080<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes, one search, no `n`.

`^` and `$` are anchors. They do not consume a character; they assert
where the match is allowed to sit. `^PORT` matches `PORT` only when
there is nothing before it on the line, and `=$` matches an equals sign
only when there is nothing after it — which in an env file is exactly
"a key with no value", a useful thing to be able to search for when the
deploy fails with a blank config.

Anchors are the cheapest precision there is. Half the false matches you
will ever get come from a short pattern that also lives inside longer
words — `PORT` inside `DB_PORT`, `id` inside `paid`, `test` inside
`latest`. When the thing you want is at the start or end of a line,
one extra character removes every one of those.

You already know `^` and `$` as motions: `0` and `$` from chapter 6,
and `^` to the first non-blank. It is not a coincidence. Inside a
pattern they name the same two places.

The `$ciw` at the end is one of those pairs worth having ready. A
value at the end of a line, of unknown length: go to the end, change
the word. No counting.

@start .env
NODE_ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=info
DB_HOST=database
DB_PORT=3306
DB_NAME=orders
DB_USER=orders
RABBIT_HOST=rabbit-1
RABBIT_PORT=5672
RABBIT_VHOST=payments
METRICS_PORT=9100
SMTP_HOST=smtp.internal
SMTP_PORT=2525
PORT=4000
HOST=0.0.0.0
GATEWAY_URL=https://gateway.internal:8443
SENTRY_DSN=

@expected .env
NODE_ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=info
DB_HOST=database
DB_PORT=3306
DB_NAME=orders
DB_USER=orders
RABBIT_HOST=rabbit-1
RABBIT_PORT=5672
RABBIT_VHOST=payments
METRICS_PORT=9100
SMTP_HOST=smtp.internal
SMTP_PORT=2525
PORT=8080
HOST=0.0.0.0
GATEWAY_URL=https://gateway.internal:8443
SENTRY_DSN=
